Benjamin Lewis <bclewis@cs.sfu.ca> writes:
> The problem is that the character symbol used is considered by xterm to be
> an "unprintable character".  On the rogue level, with IBMGraphics, the
> symbol used for the character is that "smiley face" char.  I don't remember
> the exact char code, but it's somewhere below 32 (space), and so newer
> versions of xterm refuse to print it.  (It works for me if I play in the
> linux console, by the way, and it worked with an older version of xterm).

Surely it can't just be a matter of its (0x01) being below 0x20?
There's several other of the Rogue level IBMgraphics symbols that are
in that range: weapons (0x18, up-arrow), amulets (0x0c, female
symbol), food (0x05, clubs suit symbol), scrolls (0x0e, musical note),
gold and gems (0x0f, hollow star). (There are several others that are
deliberately _avoided_ because they're used as control characters; is
it just that newer xterms have added another of these?)

> Unfortunately, I don't know how to fix the problem.  My work around is
> usually to toggle IBMGraphics when I get to this level, although you can
> also get around it by riding a steed.

Showrace should work for gnomes, orcs, and dwarves, as it's only the @
symbol that's replaced. Or polymorph, of course :-) You'd still have
the problem with any @ monsters that happened to get onto the level,
mind (though they won't be generated there, not being a capital
letter).

> The most helpful advice I got on #nethack was along the lines of "well, you
> shouldn't be using this non-standard charset anyway"... :)

You could hack assign_rogue_graphics() in drawing.c not to do it; just
replace the "monsyms[S_HUMAN] = 0x01;" with something else (or comment
it out).

